# Valgrind suppressions for navi's leak check.
#
# Scope: we run with --show-leak-kinds=definite,indirect and treat only those as
# errors, so OpenSSL's process-global "still reachable" caches and "possibly
# lost" interior pointers are already ignored without suppressions. Entries here
# are for the residual *definite/indirect* leaks that are genuinely not navi's
# to free (one-time library/loader initialization). Keep this list short and
# specific; a navi-owned leak must be fixed, not suppressed.

# One-time dynamic-loader init when the first dlopen'd codec/TLS lib is resolved.
{
  dl-init-once
  Memcheck:Leak
  match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
  ...
  fun:_dl_init
}

# --track-fds also reports descriptors still open at exit (CoreError:FdNotClosed),
# which catches a leaked navi socket or cert file. The event-loop backends
# (asyncdispatch, chronos) keep their dispatcher's own control descriptors open for
# the whole process, though: the epoll instance, the eventfd/pipe used to wake the
# loop, and chronos's timer/signal fds. navi never opens these directly (its
# sockets show fun:socket / SSL frames), so suppress them by their creating
# syscall. A real leaked socket does not match and still fails the run.
{
  eventloop-epoll-fd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:epoll_create1
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-epoll-fd-legacy
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:epoll_create
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-wakeup-eventfd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:eventfd
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-wakeup-pipe
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:pipe2
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-timerfd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:timerfd_create
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-signalfd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:signalfd
  ...
}
